{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, multiplication","reason":"For positive integers r,s, r²s²=(rs)² is again a positive integer square. The other operations fail: 1+1=2, 1/4 is not an integer square, and √4=2 is not a square.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h002","cnm_019","cnm_074"],"id":"square-product-closure","label":"Prove closure and test the other operations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-product-closure":["math:operation-closure-and-laws","cnm_143"]},"nodeIds":["math:operation-closure-and-laws","cnm_143","cnm_h002","cnm_019","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: all three","reason":"Rules (1) and (2) both express properties of the maximum. For (3), assume b≤c: both sides then equal min(a,c), proving the distributive identity.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Addition and multiplication law nodes should not be substituted merely because the identities have similar formal shapes."],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:defined-binary-operation-evaluation"],"id":"reason-from-selection-rules","label":"Apply the larger and smaller selection rules","methodDirectNodeIds":{"reason-from-selection-rules":["math:operation-closure-and-laws"]},"nodeIds":["math:operation-closure-and-laws","math:defined-binary-operation-evaluation"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B","reason":"Since x*y=xy+x+y, the two sides in B differ by x. For x=1 and y=z=0, the left side is 1 and the right side is 2. The other identities hold; associativity follows from (x*y)+1=(x+1)(y+1).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"