erivedAnswer":"D, operations 1, 2, and 3","reason":"For integers u,v, 2u+2v=2(u+v), 2u-2v=2(u-v), and (2u)(2v)=2(2uv), all even. Division fails since 2/2=1, and averaging fails since (0+2)/2=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p156","cnm_h002","cnm_p129"],"id":"prove-closure-and-test-failures","label":"Prove the successful cases and give counterexamples","methodDirectNodeIds":{"prove-closure-and-test-failures":["math:operation-closure-and-laws"]},"nodeIds":["math:operation-closure-and-laws","cnm_p156","cnm_h002","cnm_p129"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, II and IV only","reason":"For m(a,b) = (a + b)/2, commutativity holds and a + m(b,c) = m(a + b,a + c). Associativity fails, averaging does not distribute over addition, and an identity would require a fixed e satisfying e = a for every a.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1973_AMC_12_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_211","cnm_036","cnm_071"],"id":"1973_AMC_12_Q05_m1","label":"Test the operation laws","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1973_AMC_12_Q05_m1":["math:operation-closure-and-laws"]},"nodeIds":["math:operation-closure-and-laws","cnm_211","cnm_036","cnm_071"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C","reason":"Here x♥0=|x|, which is not x when x is negative. For example, (-1)♥0=1≠-1. The other statements follow from basic absolute-value properties.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2003_AMC_10A_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_008","cnm_071"],"id":"absolute-value-identities","label":"Apply the absolute-value definition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"absolute-value-identitie